---
import ConicalSpring from '@/components/diagrams/ConicalSpring.astro';
import MaterialsTable from '@/components/ui/MaterialsTable.astro';
import { conical } from '@/data/products';
import { linkTo } from '@/data/routes';
import ProductLayout from '@/layouts/ProductLayout.astro';

const compression = linkTo('compression');
---

<ProductLayout product={conical}>
  <Fragment slot="about">
    <p>
      A conical spring is a compression spring whose coil diameter <strong>tapers</strong> from a
      large end to a small end. As it is pressed, the larger coils can seat over the smaller ones,
      so the solid height can be only a few wire diameters plus the closed ends.
    </p>
    <p>
      The rate is not a single number. It usually rises as more of the large coils go solid. That is
      useful on a battery contact or a push-button, where you want a soft start and a firmer finish.
      It is a poor choice if you need a linear rate over a long stroke — use a{' '}
      {compression ? (
        <a href={compression}>cylindrical compression spring</a>
      ) : (
        'cylindrical compression spring'
      )}{' '}
      for that.
    </p>
    <p>
      Same wire range as the rest of our unit: 0.40–2.50 mm. Same materials. Ends can be open,
      closed or ground, independently at each end if the drawing says so.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="types">
    <figure class="type-fig max-w-[28rem]">
      <div class="bp-grid type-draw">
        <ConicalSpring annotated />
      </div>
      <figcaption>Large OD, small OD and free length — the three numbers we quote from.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose mt-6 max-w-[46rem]">
      <p>
        A barrel (convex) or hourglass (concave) is a cousin of the cone. Send the drawing. If the
        part is a simple taper in our wire range, that is a regular job.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Fragment>

  <MaterialsTable slot="materials" />

  <Fragment slot="tips">
    <p>
      Give <strong>both outer diameters</strong> and the free length. Say which end sits in the
      pocket and which end sits on the pin. If the large end must not pass through a hole, say so —
      that is a go / no-go dimension, not a decoration.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="applications">
    <p>
      Battery contacts, push-buttons, light switchgear, and any pocket that is wide at one end and
      tight at the other. Instrumentation and small automotive switches use this shape when a
      straight spring would stack too tall.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ProductLayout>

<style>
  .type-fig {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .type-draw {
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-grid);
    border-radius: var(--radius-card);
    color: var(--color-navy-900);
  }

  figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
